<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Przemyśl</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Peremyshl</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Przemyśl is a city on the San river in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, near the Ukrainian border. One of the Cherven towns, it was ruled by Rus' princes from the 11th century and was the seat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volodar-rostyslavych">Volodar Rostyslavych</a>, then part of the principalities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> and Galicia-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a> before passing to Poland in the 1340s. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> it became a great fortress, besieged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1914 and 1915.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/przemysl.jpg" alt="Przemyśl" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ferdziu · CC BY-SA 3.0 pl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bazylika_archikatedralna_Wniebowzi%C4%99cia_Naj%C5%9Bwi%C4%99tszej_Maryi_Panny2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Przemyśl lies on the navigable San River, in an area connecting mountains and lowlands known as the Przemyśl Gate. Open lines of transport and fertile soil gave it importance, and trade routes link it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>. It is the second-oldest city in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, dating back to the 8th century, when a fortified gord of the Lendians, a West Slavic tribe, stood on the site. In the 9th century it became part of Great Moravia, and its name most likely dates from that period.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The region was contested between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. Mieszko I annexed the Lendian lands in 970 to 980, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nestor">Nestor</a> the Chronicler recorded the city's capture by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-the-great">Vladimir I of Kiev</a> in 981. It changed hands several times in the 11th century, and in 1085 became the capital of a semi-independent Principality of Peremyshl under Kievan Rus'. An <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> eparchy was founded there before 1218, and from 1246 it was under Mongol suzerainty. In 1340 Casimir III of Poland retook it, and its charter on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> rights was confirmed in 1389.</p><p>The city prospered as a trade centre in the 16th century, with a population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, Germans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechs">Czechs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruthenians">Ruthenians</a>. The Old Synagogue dates from 1559 and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> college from 1617. The Swedish invasion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-deluge">Deluge</a> and the general decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> ended this prosperity in the mid-17th century. In 1772 Przemyśl passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-partition-of-poland">First Partition of Poland</a>. Between 1888 and 1914 it was made a first-class fortress with 44 forts. The Russians besieged it in October 1914, and it surrendered on 22 March 1915 after the fortifications were destroyed; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a> retook it on 3 June 1915.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In November 1918 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukrainians">Ukrainians</a> fought for the city, and Polish forces crossed the San on 11 and 12 November. In September 1939 the German army took it, and the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> ran along the San through the city until June 1941. Over 500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were murdered in and around the city that September. A ghetto was established on 15 July 1942, holding some 22,000 people, and until September 1943 almost all its Jews were sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiecim">Auschwitz</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belzec-extermination-camp">Belzec</a>. Major Max Liedtke and Albert Battel were later named Righteous Among the Nations by Yad Vashem. After 1945 the border lay about 15 kilometres east of the city.</p><p>Przemyśl is in the Subcarpathian Voivodeship. Its Old Town is listed as a Historic Monument of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, as is the Przemyśl Fortress. Sights include the castle built by Casimir III in the 14th century, the cathedral and the Carmelite church.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Przemyśl” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>That year it was sung in <b>Przemyśl</b>, then in the Austrian Kingdom of Galicia and Lodomeria, at a commemoration of Shevchenko; this performance is the origin of Ukraine's National Anthem Day, held each year on 10 March.</blockquote><a href="/songs/national-anthem-of-ukraine">State Anthem of Ukraine</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 1080s three Rurikid brothers, Rurik, Volodar and Vasylko Rostislavich, took control of the towns of <b>Peremyshl</b>, Zvenyhorod and Terebovl in the far south-west of Rus'.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-galicia">Principality of Galicia</a></li></ul></section>
